rules_version = '2';
// MYB Roster — Firestore Security Rules
//
// ── SWEEP v20.12: the Calendar stops being public, and the `calendarViewer` audit ───────────────
//
// `overrides` read was `allow read;` — no auth of any kind — and is now a `name` claim (a real
// member) OR the `calendarViewer` capability (the shared staff PIN, minted server-side by the
// unlockCalendarViewer function). Anonymous is DENIED, which is the substance of the change: the
// Calendar used to sign every visitor in anonymously, so `request.auth != null` here would have
// re-admitted precisely the people it closes out.
//
// A new authenticated identity means every `request.auth != null` in this file had to be re-read,
// because each one silently widened. The audit, in full — do this again if another capability is
// ever added, and do not skip a collection because it "obviously" does not matter:
//
//   COLLECTION          VIEWER GETS            WHY THAT IS RIGHT
//   overrides           READ only              the point of the feature; writes need name/admin/manager
//   huddles             read (already open)    unchanged — open by design, notification taps have no session
//   circulars           read (already open)    unchanged, same reason
//   newsletters         read (already open)    unchanged, same reason
//   linkDesigns         nothing                needs `name` or admin (v19.39) — the viewer has neither
//   linkTargetSets      nothing                same read as linkDesigns; write per-creator (v21.04)
//   staffContact        nothing                owner (`name`) or admin
//   passwordStatus      nothing                owner (`name`) or admin
//   resetRequests       nothing                admin read, server-only write
//   clientErrors        WRITE only             deliberate: an office PC hitting a bug should still be
//                                              able to report it. Read/update stay admin-only, so the
//                                              viewer can post an error and never read one.
//   analytics           WRITE only             deliberate: usage from shared PCs is real usage. The
//                                              writes are counters, carry no identity, and read is
//                                              admin-only. Same posture anonymous sessions had.
//   pushSubscriptions   nothing (TIGHTENED)    every office PC shares one uid, so a subscription
//                                              written under it is owned by fifty people — see the block
//   viewerAttempts      nothing (NEW)          the throttle store; server-only in both directions
//
// Sweep v20.56: `overtimeWindows` added (Overtime Availability). Reviewer read, no client writes,
// one posture for the whole tree — the viewer and every ordinary member get nothing, which is why
// it needed no new line in the audit table above beyond this one. Two things are deliberately NOT
// in that block and the reasons are in it: a participant-based member-read rule (no production
// client would exercise it) and a retention filter (rules are not filters, and it could only ever
// constrain the reviewer, who is entitled to the data).
// Last swept v21.04 (Aug 2026): NEW `linkTargetSets` collection (saved generator target sets) —
// designer create with createdBy pinned to the writer's own name claim; update/delete only by the
// creator or admin, createdBy immutable on update. Read matches linkDesigns.
// Prior sweep v19.41: linkDesigns create/update accepts the optional `deletedAt`/
// `deletedBy` soft-delete pair (present = in the bin, absent = live), type-checked when present.
// Sweep v19.95: the analytics pv_ counts allowlist gains `guide-staff` and `guide-paycalc` — the
// two guides that had no open counter, so the "Documents & guides" group silently answered a
// narrower question than it appeared to. Prior sweep v19.39: linkDesigns READ now requires a `name` claim — the previous
// `request.auth != null` admitted the calendar's anonymous sessions, i.e. any visitor, which the
// rule's own comment did not say. Prior sweep v19.23: analytics gains the `origins` doc (per-address migration counters —
// a NEW doc rather than a third map on activeAccounts, so a rules-deploy lag cannot deny the writes
// that already work). Prior sweep v18.20: analytics pv_ counts allowlist gains the five document/guide open ids
// (huddle/circular/newsletter/guide-railcard/guide-fip). Prior sweep v17.03: B3 strict override
// isolation · override update-owner re-check (v16.80) ·
// H2 linksDesigner writes + linkDesigns shape-validation (v17.02) · analytics anti-wipe map guard
// (v17.03) · staffContact Chiltern work-email validation (v16.34 local-part tightening) · 20 MB
// inclusive upload cap lives in storage.rules · analytics doc-id pinning.
// (This file is NOT covered by the 0.10 doc-stamp sweep — update this line by hand when rules change.)
//
// Write policy: PER-MEMBER ISOLATION is now STRICT (B3, see SECURITY_RELEASE_PLAN.md).
// `overrides` create/update/delete require the member's own `name` claim, OR an `admin`/`manager`
// claim (both write on behalf). The B2 permissive `!('name' in token)` legacy/anonymous escape has
// been REMOVED (B3 strict cutover) after the CLAIM_EPOCH=2 forced re-auth sweep + a re-provision.
// A stale token that has not refreshed since the sweep is self-healed on its first write by the
// writeWithClaimRetry net (v15.18: permission-denied → force token refresh → retry). All other
// collections keep their existing posture; field validation is enforced everywhere for integrity.
// NOTE the claim prerequisite: managers must be provisioned (Operations → Set up accounts, which
// sets the `manager` claim) AND token-refreshed before this isolation rule is relied upon.
//
// To re-deploy rules: push a change to this file on main; deploy-rules.yml runs
// automatically. Or: GitHub → Actions → "Deploy Firestore Rules" → Run workflow.
//
// Last updated: v16.29 — B3 STRICT + H2 linkDesigns cutover (two rules deploys on the 16.29 line).
//   B3: removed the `!('name' in token)` no-name escape from the overrides create/update AND delete
//   blocks (only that line changed; the name/admin/manager 3-tier is retained). Shipped after
//   re-provision + the CLAIM_EPOCH=2 sweep. Rollback = re-add the two escape lines and redeploy.
//   H2 (this deploy): linkDesigns write now requires the `linksDesigner` claim (or admin); reads were
//   left open to any authed session (tightened to a `name` claim at v19.39 — see the sweep line at the
//   top of this file). setupRosterAuth sets linksDesigner for CONFIG.LINKS_DESIGNERS; a stale
//   designer token self-heals via writeWithClaimRetry in links-app.js. Rollback = restore
//   `allow read, write: if request.auth != null;`. Both rollbacks are instant, no data migration.
//   clientErrors pins `timestamp == request.time` (server clock), admin-only read/update.
//   Override `value` grammar accepts the other-family sentinels (TRG/IND/ASSESS/TEAM ± RDW ± times).
//   staffContact owner+admin; analytics any-auth write / admin read; pushSubscriptions
//   shape-validated, delete auth-only.
// v14.53 — B2 per-member override isolation (permissive 3-tier: name/admin/manager
//   + no-name escape), override delete tightened to match, override/circular/newsletter dates
//   bounded to real month/day ranges. linkDesigns deliberately left open (see its block).
// v14.29 — huddles create/update brought to parity with circulars/newsletters:
//   fileType constrained to ['pdf','docx'], uploadedBy required-as-string, and htmlContent
//   type-checked (`is string`) before its 250 KB size cap. staffContact + pushSubscriptions
//   were already fully hardened (hasOnly + field types) in v14.24 — no change needed there.
// v14.24 — schema tightening (deferred v14.11-review bug-class backlog):
//   overrides hasAll→hasOnly (verified 8-field allowlist incl. createdAt/changedBy; no path
//   writes other fields), date must match YYYY-MM-DD, shift/rdw time bounded to 00-23:00-59
//   (rejects impossible times like 99:99), createdAt required-as-timestamp; pushSubscriptions
//   nested keys map restricted to exactly p256dh+auth. DEFERRED-then (need owner input): staffContact
//   work-email DOMAIN enforcement (exact Chiltern domain unconfirmed → lockout risk) — **SHIPPED
//   later, v14.97** (see the staffContact block, `@chilternrailways.co.uk`); local-part tightened to
//   reject whitespace v16.34. Still open: the circular/newsletter/huddle storagePath prefix check (low value).
// v12.79 — staffContact create/update hardened: hasOnly (not hasAll) so
//   clients can't add extra fields (e.g. a future server-only `verified`); updatedAt must be a timestamp.
// v12.68 — staffContact collection added (owner-only read/write via name claim).
// v12.04 — overrides read required auth (reverted); pushSubscriptions delete requires auth;
//   legacy override types removed from create/update allowlist.
// v11.92 — pushSubscriptions shape validation added.
// v10.94 — revert per-member claim requirement to auth-only.
// v10.83 — restrict huddle writes to admin; fix Storage rules to allow admin uploads.
// v10.72 — member write isolation (v11 task #2). Suspended — see note above.

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {

    match /overrides/{document=**} {
      // ── READ REQUIRES A REAL IDENTITY (v20.12 — was `allow read;`, fully public) ──────────────
      //
      // Override data is annual leave, absence and shift changes for fifty named colleagues, and
      // until now anyone who knew the app URL could read all of it with no credential at all. The
      // note this replaced defended that as "the URL is not publicly advertised", which is not a
      // control — it is the absence of one.
      //
      // TWO identities qualify, and the second is the reason this could finally be closed:
      //   · a real MYB member — a `name` claim, set by setupRosterAuth after a password sign-in;
      //   · the shared staff Calendar viewer — `calendarViewer`, minted by the unlockCalendarViewer
      //     Cloud Function in exchange for the staff PIN. Staff open the roster on shared office
      //     PCs for a thirty-second look, and requiring a full member sign-in for that would have
      //     made this change cost more usability than it bought security.
      //
      // ANONYMOUS IS DENIED, and that is the whole point: the Calendar used to sign every visitor
      // in anonymously, so `request.auth != null` here would have re-admitted exactly the people
      // this closes out — the same trap the linkDesigns read rule fell into until v19.39, where a
      // rule that read as "any signed-in member" in fact meant "anybody who can open the app".
      // `admin` is listed for symmetry with that rule; claimsForTier gives every admin a `name` too.
      //
      // ⚠️ THE RULE IS NOT THE WHOLE GATE. Firestore evaluates rules on the SERVER, so a LOCAL
      // IndexedDB cache hit never reaches this line — a browser that unlocked yesterday still holds
      // every override it saw. calendar-overrides.js refuses those reads separately
      // (`setOverrideAccess`). Tightening this rule without that gate would look secure and leak.
      //
      // MEASURED, not inherited (Aug 2026): experiments/firestore-offline-proof/ demonstrates it
      // against a real browser cache — cached overrides read back in full while the server read on
      // the same query returns permission-denied, and they survive a full browser restart. A denied
      // LISTEN does not evict them either. So closing this rule protects the roster on devices that
      // have not already seen it, and does nothing at all about devices that have.
      //
      // ENFORCED 26 Aug 2026 (v20.16 → today). The `allow read;` hold line that sat here is gone,
      // and with it the last route to override data without a credential. The hold existed so the
      // client gate could ship first and soak — the three deploy workflows fire in parallel from
      // one push, so shipping both together would have let a rules deploy land before the hosting
      // one and show every staff phone the BASE ROSTER under a "Couldn't update" chip: a roster
      // that is wrong rather than obviously broken.
      //
      // Closed on the runbook's own criteria (RECOVERY_RUNBOOK.md → "The Calendar PIN" step 4):
      // the App Speed version breakdown showed most real loads had moved off the pre-v20.51
      // client, and page views were broadly level with July, which is the signal that unlocks are
      // NOT silently failing — a locked Calendar has no identity, so a failed PIN exchange never
      // reaches the Error Log and silence there proves nothing.
      //
      // FROM HERE, `CALENDAR_PIN_ACCESS: false` IS NO LONGER A ROLLBACK. The server denies these
      // reads whatever the client asks for, so the rollback is reverting this change and
      // redeploying rules — a two-minute path, but a different one from the flag flip.
      allow read: if request.auth != null && (
        'name' in request.auth.token ||
        request.auth.token.admin == true ||
        request.auth.token.calendarViewer == true
      );
      allow create, update: if
        request.auth != null &&
        // hasOnly (not hasAll) — reject any field beyond the known override shape.
        // The complete set written by every path is verified: all three write paths
        // (manual save, AL/sick range writer, roster_import) write changedBy and
        // createdAt. No path writes updatedAt/updatedBy. (Field PRESENCE of the six core
        // fields is still enforced by the `is string` checks below — a missing field is
        // null, and `null is string` is false.)
        request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(
          ['date', 'memberName', 'type', 'value', 'note', 'source', 'createdAt', 'changedBy',
           'replacedType']
        ) &&
        request.resource.data.date is string &&
        request.resource.data.date.size() == 10 &&
        // Bounded YYYY-MM-DD: month 01-12, day 01-31 (matches() is full-string in RE2, so this
        // is anchored). Rejects impossible ISO-shaped dates like 2026-99-99 / 2026-13-01 that the
        // old unbounded [0-9]{2} pattern accepted. NOTE: it does NOT reject a well-formed but
        // non-existent day (2026-02-31, or 2026-02-29 in a non-leap year). The strict real-calendar
        // parse (parseStrictIsoDate) only guards the FUNCTION paths (Huddle/roster ingest); ordinary
        // browser override writes reach Firestore directly, so on THIS path such a day is accepted at
        // the Rules boundary. Accepted low-risk integrity gap — the app's own date pickers can't emit
        // one; a leap-year algorithm in Rules (with emulator coverage) isn't worth it for this team.
        request.resource.data.date.matches('[0-9]{4}-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12][0-9]|3[01])') &&
        request.resource.data.memberName is string &&
        request.resource.data.memberName.size() > 0 &&
        request.resource.data.memberName.size() < 100 &&
        request.resource.data.type in [
          'spare_shift', 'shift', 'rdw',
          'annual_leave', 'correction', 'sick', 'other'
        ] &&
        request.resource.data.source in ['manual', 'roster_import'] &&
        // type↔value consistency: value format must match the override type.
        // Prevents any authenticated user writing garbage shift data for other members.
        // The fixed-value contract mirrors TYPES[*].fixedValue in admin-overrides.js
        // (spare_shift→SPARE, annual_leave→AL, correction→RD, sick→SICK); only the
        // timed types (shift, rdw) carry an HH:MM-HH:MM range. The importer normalises
        // every correction (RD/OFF/Sunday AL/SICK) to 'RD' before writing.
        // 'other' (OTHER_PLAN.md) uses the grammar FLAVOUR[" RDW"][" HH:MM-HH:MM"]
        // — flavour TRG/IND/ASSESS/TEAM/UNION/MEET, optional rest-day marker, optional actual times
        // (bounded, so an impossible time can never ride in on a training value).
        // Keep this alternation in sync with _OTHER_RE in override-utils.js.
        (
          (request.resource.data.type in ['shift', 'rdw'] &&
           // Bounded HH:MM-HH:MM — hours 00-23, minutes 00-59 (rejects impossible times
           // like 99:99 that the unbounded [0-9]{2} pattern previously accepted).
           request.resource.data.value.matches('([01][0-9]|2[0-3]):[0-5][0-9]-([01][0-9]|2[0-3]):[0-5][0-9]')) ||
          (request.resource.data.type == 'spare_shift'  && request.resource.data.value == 'SPARE') ||
          (request.resource.data.type == 'annual_leave' && request.resource.data.value == 'AL') ||
          (request.resource.data.type == 'correction'   && request.resource.data.value == 'RD') ||
          (request.resource.data.type == 'sick'         && request.resource.data.value == 'SICK') ||
          (request.resource.data.type == 'other'     &&
           request.resource.data.value.matches('(TRG|IND|ASSESS|TEAM|UNION|MEET)( RDW)?( ([01][0-9]|2[0-3]):[0-5][0-9]-([01][0-9]|2[0-3]):[0-5][0-9])?'))
        ) &&
        request.resource.data.note is string &&
        request.resource.data.note.size() < 500 &&
        // createdAt is written by every path (serverTimestamp); changedBy only by the
        // manual/AL/sick paths — validate its type when present without requiring it.
        request.resource.data.createdAt is timestamp &&
        (!request.resource.data.keys().hasAny(['changedBy']) || (request.resource.data.changedBy is string && request.resource.data.changedBy.size() <= 100)) &&
        // ── replacedType — OPTIONAL, and the only surviving record of a swapped day ─────────
        //
        // A write is delete-then-set, so recording annual leave DESTROYS the override that said
        // what the day was. On a swapped-in day (a `shift` sitting on a base rest day) that
        // deleted doc was the only evidence the member was contracted to work it, and without it
        // the entitlement check falls back to the base roster, sees a rest day and charges
        // nothing — the leave is free. So the write carries forward what it replaced.
        //
        // Constrained to the same type vocabulary as `type` itself: it names an override that
        // existed, so anything outside that set is meaningless. Absent on most docs (nothing was
        // replaced) and on every doc written before v21.55, which is why it is validated only
        // when present — the readers treat its absence as "ask the base roster", i.e. exactly the
        // behaviour every existing document already has.
        (!request.resource.data.keys().hasAny(['replacedType']) || (
          request.resource.data.replacedType in [
            'spare_shift', 'shift', 'rdw',
            'annual_leave', 'correction', 'sick', 'other',
            'allocated', 'overtime', 'swap'
          ]
        )) &&
        // ── Per-member write isolation (B3, STRICT form) ──────────────────────────────────
        // A staff member may write only their OWN override (token.name == the doc's memberName).
        // Two load-bearing bypasses: ADMIN writes for everyone (AL/sick on behalf + every
        // source:'roster_import' row) and MANAGER does the same on-behalf editing daily.
        // The B2 permissive `!('name' in token)` legacy/anonymous escape has been REMOVED
        // (B3 strict cutover) after the CLAIM_EPOCH=2 forced re-auth sweep. Managers must be
        // provisioned (Set up accounts) AND token-refreshed before this rule is relied upon —
        // a stale manager token has name but not manager, so it would be isolated to its own
        // (non-existent) roster. See SECURITY_RELEASE_PLAN.md → B3.
        // TWO checks, because create AND update share this block. On UPDATE the EXISTING doc's
        // owner must also authorise (resource.data.memberName), not just the NEW value — otherwise a
        // member could OVERWRITE another member's override by writing their own name into it,
        // silently re-labelling and destroying the victim's record (the update twin of the hole the
        // delete rule below closes). resource == null on create, so the existing-owner clause is a
        // no-op there. (v16.80 review fix.)
        (
          resource == null ||
          request.auth.token.name == resource.data.memberName ||
          request.auth.token.admin == true ||
          request.auth.token.manager == true
        ) &&
        (
          request.auth.token.name == request.resource.data.memberName ||
          request.auth.token.admin == true ||
          request.auth.token.manager == true
        );
      // Delete mirrors the same three-tier STRICT check against the EXISTING doc's
      // memberName (request.resource is null on delete). Previously any authenticated user
      // could delete any override — isolation on writes is moot without it.
      allow delete: if request.auth != null && (
        request.auth.token.name == resource.data.memberName ||
        request.auth.token.admin == true ||
        request.auth.token.manager == true
      );
    }

    match /huddles/{date} {
      // Open read: index.html reads huddles without a Firebase Auth session —
      // required for Huddle button and notification auto-open on fresh visits.
      allow read;
      // Cloud Function writes use Admin SDK — bypasses these rules entirely.
      // Browser writes (manual admin upload in operations.html) require admin claim.
      // Non-admin staff cannot alter huddle metadata even if authenticated.
      allow create, update: if
        request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.token.admin == true &&
        // hasOnly prevents clients from writing arbitrary extra fields (e.g. injecting
        // script into htmlContent beyond the 250 KB cap). uploadedAt must be a server
        // timestamp so the display order is authoritative.
        request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['date', 'storageUrl', 'storagePath', 'fileType', 'uploadedAt', 'uploadedBy', 'htmlContent']) &&
        request.resource.data.date is string &&
        // Bound the date to a real YYYY-MM-DD, matching circulars/newsletters. A malformed
        // date would break pruneOldHuddles' string-range (`date < cutoffISO`) prune, leaving
        // that doc + its Storage object un-pruned indefinitely.
        request.resource.data.date.size() == 10 &&
        request.resource.data.date.matches('[0-9]{4}-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12][0-9]|3[01])') &&
        request.resource.data.storageUrl is string &&
        // Browser writes (uploadHuddle) set fileType to the short form 'pdf'|'docx'.
        // The Cloud Function ingest path uses the Admin SDK and bypasses these rules.
        request.resource.data.fileType in ['pdf', 'docx'] &&
        request.resource.data.uploadedBy is string &&
        request.resource.data.uploadedAt is timestamp &&
        // htmlContent is optional; when present it must be a string AND within the 250 KB cap.
        // Checking `is string` first guards .size() from being called on a non-string value.
        (!('htmlContent' in request.resource.data) ||
         (request.resource.data.htmlContent is string && request.resource.data.htmlContent.size() <= 250000));
      allow delete: if request.auth != null && request.auth.token.admin == true;
    }

    match /linkDesigns/{document=**} {
      // Read requires a NAMED identity — a session that has actually signed in as a member —
      // not merely `request.auth != null` (v19.39). The old rule was described as "any
      // authenticated session", which read as "any signed-in member", but calendar-app.js calls
      // signInAnonymously() unconditionally on every visit: an anonymous session satisfied it, so
      // anyone who could open the app URL could read every design straight from the API. The
      // sensitivity judgement itself still holds — staff names were removed from designs at v12.39,
      // leaving line numbers and shift times — but "non-sensitive to a colleague" is a different
      // claim from "non-sensitive to anyone who finds the URL", and only the first was ever
      // intended.
      // Deliberately NOT narrowed to the linksDesigner claim: that is exactly the lockout the open
      // read existed to prevent — a designer whose token predates the claim must still be able to
      // LOAD the workspace, so that their first write can permission-deny and self-heal through
      // writeWithClaimRetry. Requiring `name` keeps that escape hatch (every provisioned member has
      // it) while excluding the anonymous sessions that were never meant to be included.
      allow read: if request.auth != null &&
        ('name' in request.auth.token || request.auth.token.admin == true);
      // Writes require the linksDesigner claim (or admin, who outranks). setupRosterAuth
      // sets linksDesigner:true for CONFIG.LINKS_DESIGNERS. Closes H2 — before this, the
      // links-app.js client redirect was the ONLY control and the server accepted a write
      // from any authenticated session. See SECURITY_RELEASE_PLAN.md → H2.
      // A designer on a pre-cutover token self-heals: links-app.js wraps every linkDesigns
      // write in writeWithClaimRetry (v16.29), so a permission-denied forces a token refresh
      // and one retry — no manual sign-out needed.
      function isLinksWriter() {
        return request.auth != null &&
          (request.auth.token.linksDesigner == true || request.auth.token.admin == true);
      }
      // Delete carries no body to validate (v17.02 — Finding #12), but it DOES have state to
      // check (v19.84, external review P1): a hard delete is only ever legitimate against a
      // design already in the bin. The client purges through a transaction that re-reads the
      // server copy, so a design somebody restored a moment ago is spared — but that is a client
      // rule, and this collection is writable by three designers at once. Requiring `deletedAt`
      // here means a future client carrying the same bug it took a review to find cannot destroy
      // a LIVE design; it can only fail. Soft-delete itself is an UPDATE and is unaffected.
      allow delete: if isLinksWriter()
        && ('deletedAt' in resource.data)
        && resource.data.deletedAt is timestamp;
      // Create/update are SHAPE-VALIDATED (v17.02 — Finding #12): linkDesigns was the last
      // designer-writable collection with no schema check, so a designer/admin (or a bug on
      // their session) could write an arbitrary/oversized document. hasOnly rejects any field
      // beyond the known four; the `is` checks reject wrong types AND a missing field (a
      // missing key reads as null, and `null is string` is false). `patterns` is only
      // type-checked as a map — its nested {"1".."28":{sun..sat}} shape isn't practical to
      // validate in rules, and the 1 MB document limit already bounds its size. The rename
      // path's merge:true write still satisfies hasOnly because request.resource.data reflects
      // the full post-merge document (existing patterns + the new name/updatedAt/updatedBy).
      // `deletedAt`/`deletedBy` are the v19.41 SOFT DELETE: present = in the bin, absent = live.
      // Both are OPTIONAL — a live document must not be forced to carry them (every existing
      // design predates the field, and hasOnly is about the ceiling, not the floor). When present
      // they are type-checked like everything else. A restore clears them with deleteField(), so
      // the resulting document has neither key and passes this rule unchanged.
      // NOTE the rules cannot express "only a designer may empty the bin" any more finely than
      // this: delete is already gated on isLinksWriter(), which is the same set of people who can
      // soft-delete. The retention window is a CLIENT policy (links-deletion.js), not a server one
      // — worth knowing before treating the 30 days as a guarantee.
      // `window` is the v19.54 OPERATING WINDOW — when the station is staffed, stored per design
      // so a proposal can test a moved boundary (the live case is Sunday, where five December 2026
      // movements fall after the current 23:25 finish). OPTIONAL, like deletedAt/deletedBy: every
      // design predates it and reads as the app default. Type-checked as a map only — its
      // {monSat,sun}:{start,end} shape is validated client-side by links-window.js's
      // normaliseWindow, which is also the read path, so a malformed value can never reach the UI
      // as anything but the default.
      allow create, update: if isLinksWriter() &&
        request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['name', 'patterns', 'window', 'updatedAt', 'updatedBy', 'deletedAt', 'deletedBy']) &&
        request.resource.data.name is string &&
        request.resource.data.name.size() >= 1 &&
        request.resource.data.name.size() <= 100 &&
        request.resource.data.patterns is map &&
        request.resource.data.updatedAt is timestamp &&
        request.resource.data.updatedBy is string &&
        request.resource.data.updatedBy.size() <= 100 &&
        (!('deletedAt' in request.resource.data) || request.resource.data.deletedAt is timestamp) &&
        (!('window' in request.resource.data) || request.resource.data.window is map) &&
        (!('deletedBy' in request.resource.data) ||
          (request.resource.data.deletedBy is string && request.resource.data.deletedBy.size() <= 100));
    }

    match /linkTargetSets/{document=**} {
      // SAVED GENERATOR TARGET SETS (v21.04). Owner: "I want others to be able to mess about but
      // not lose my shift times set." The collection carries ONE rule beyond what linkDesigns
      // already has, and this block exists to state it server-side: anyone who can write designs
      // can CREATE a set, but OVERWRITING or DELETING one belongs to its creator (or the admin).
      // The client's Save button asks the same question (canOverwriteTargetSet in
      // links-target-sets.js) — that copy drives the UI; THIS one is the protection.
      //
      // Read matches linkDesigns exactly, for the same self-heal reason documented there.
      allow read: if request.auth != null &&
        ('name' in request.auth.token || request.auth.token.admin == true);

      function isLinksWriter() {
        return request.auth != null &&
          (request.auth.token.linksDesigner == true || request.auth.token.admin == true);
      }
      function validShape() {
        return request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['name', 'slots', 'spareLines', 'createdBy', 'updatedBy', 'updatedAt']) &&
          request.resource.data.name is string &&
          request.resource.data.name.size() >= 1 &&
          request.resource.data.name.size() <= 60 &&
          request.resource.data.slots is list &&
          request.resource.data.slots.size() >= 1 &&
          request.resource.data.slots.size() <= 60 &&
          request.resource.data.spareLines is int &&
          request.resource.data.spareLines >= 0 &&
          request.resource.data.spareLines < 30 &&
          request.resource.data.createdBy is string &&
          request.resource.data.createdBy.size() >= 1 &&
          request.resource.data.createdBy.size() <= 100 &&
          request.resource.data.updatedBy is string &&
          request.resource.data.updatedBy.size() <= 100 &&
          request.resource.data.updatedAt is timestamp;
      }
      // CREATE pins `createdBy` to the writer's own `name` claim — ownership cannot be forged at
      // birth. The admin is held to the same pin: an admin-created set is owned by the admin's own
      // name, not by whoever they might type, because a forged owner is a set someone ELSE can now
      // never protect (they did not create it, yet it wears their name).
      allow create: if isLinksWriter() && validShape() &&
        request.resource.data.createdBy == request.auth.token.name;
      // UPDATE requires being the creator (or admin) AND leaves `createdBy` where it was —
      // ownership is not transferable by editing. Without the immutability clause, the creator
      // check would hold only until the first overwrite rewrote the owner field.
      allow update: if isLinksWriter() && validShape() &&
        (resource.data.createdBy == request.auth.token.name || request.auth.token.admin == true) &&
        request.resource.data.createdBy == resource.data.createdBy;
      // DELETE: creator or admin. No body to validate.
      allow delete: if isLinksWriter() &&
        (resource.data.createdBy == request.auth.token.name || request.auth.token.admin == true);
    }

    match /staffContact/{memberName} {
      // Each member can read and write only their own record.
      // Admin can read all records (needed for future password recovery flows).
      // Write requires the `name` JWT claim — set by setupRosterAuth. Anonymous
      // fallback sessions lack this claim and cannot register contact details.
      allow read: if request.auth != null &&
        (request.auth.token.name == memberName || request.auth.token.admin == true);
      allow create, update: if
        request.auth != null &&
        (request.auth.token.name == memberName || request.auth.token.admin == true) &&
        // hasOnly (not hasAll) — a client must not be able to write extra fields
        // (e.g. a future server-only `verified: true`). See ROADMAP.md password stages.
        request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['memberName', 'workEmail', 'updatedAt']) &&
        request.resource.data.memberName == memberName &&
        request.resource.data.workEmail is string &&
        request.resource.data.workEmail.size() >= 5 &&
        request.resource.data.workEmail.size() <= 200 &&
        // Must be a Chiltern work email — exactly the @chilternrailways.co.uk domain
        // (case-insensitive; the "@" + anchors reject look-alikes and subdomains). The local part
        // is `[^\s@]+` (NO whitespace) to mirror the client validator isChilternWorkEmail() /
        // isValidEmail() in roster-data.js (`/^[^\s@]+@.../`) — keep the two in sync.
        request.resource.data.workEmail.matches('(?i)^[^\\s@]+@chilternrailways\\.co\\.uk$') &&
        request.resource.data.updatedAt is timestamp;
      allow delete: if request.auth != null &&
        (request.auth.token.name == memberName || request.auth.token.admin == true);
    }

    // passwordStatus/{memberName} — the per-member password-migration record (PASSWORD_PLAN.md §6).
    //   resetAt       : written ONLY by the resetMemberPassword Cloud Function (Admin SDK bypasses
    //                   these rules) — no client path can set or change it.
    //   passwordSetAt : the member stamps their OWN record after a successful updatePassword.
    // Migrated ⇔ passwordSetAt newer than any resetAt. Read = owner or admin (the Operations
    // account-status table). A member may only touch `passwordSetAt`, pinned to the server clock so
    // a wrong device clock can't fake ordering against the server-written resetAt.
    // ACCEPTED LIMITATION: `passwordSetAt` is a MONITORING signal, not a security boundary. Firestore
    // rules cannot attest that a Firebase Auth password was actually changed (an Auth-side fact), so a
    // member could self-stamp `passwordSetAt` without changing their password and appear "migrated" in
    // the admin table. This only misleads the admin about that member's OWN posture (no privilege is
    // gained), the app only ever writes it via setOwnPassword (after a real updatePassword), and the
    // real control is the surname-default retirement gate (C5), not this flag. Do not treat the
    // account-status table as a security assertion — it is a migration-progress aid.
    match /passwordStatus/{memberName} {
      allow read: if request.auth != null &&
        (request.auth.token.name == memberName || request.auth.token.admin == true);
      allow create: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.token.name == memberName &&
        request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['passwordSetAt']) &&
        request.resource.data.passwordSetAt == request.time;
      allow update: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.token.name == memberName &&
        // ONLY passwordSetAt may change — resetAt (and any other field) is immutable to clients.
        request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['passwordSetAt']) &&
        request.resource.data.passwordSetAt == request.time;
      allow delete: if false;   // migration history is server-managed, never client-deleted
    }

    // resetRequests — a locked-out member's "please reset my password" request.
    //
    // NO CLIENT MAY WRITE, AT ALL. The only writer is the `requestPasswordReset` Cloud Function via the
    // Admin SDK (which bypasses rules), because the member who needs this has no Firebase identity to
    // authenticate with — they cannot sign in, and no anonymous session exists on a protected page. So
    // rather than open a collection to unauthenticated writes, the endpoint validates server-side and
    // this stays completely closed. That is the whole reason the feature is a function and not a write.
    //
    // Doc ID is the member name, so this collection can never exceed the roster size.
    match /resetRequests/{memberName} {
      allow read:   if request.auth != null && request.auth.token.admin == true;
      allow create: if false;   // Admin SDK only (requestPasswordReset)
      allow update: if false;   // Admin SDK only
      // The admin clears a row once they have actioned it.
      allow delete: if request.auth != null && request.auth.token.admin == true;
    }

    match /clientErrors/{docId} {
      // Written by error-reporter.js on authenticated pages (request.auth != null).
      // Read, update (resolve), and delete restricted to admins.
      // Every string field is type- and size-checked so a modified client cannot
      // submit oversized values in any of them (caps mirror the client-side caps
      // in error-reporter.js). memberName is NOT bound to a token claim on purpose:
      // a degraded/anonymous session has no name claim, and we still want its
      // uncaught errors captured rather than silently rejected.
      allow create: if
        request.auth != null &&
        request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly([
          'memberName', 'page', 'message', 'stack',
          'appVersion', 'userAgent', 'timestamp', 'resolved'
        ]) &&
        request.resource.data.memberName is string &&
        request.resource.data.memberName.size() <= 100 &&
        request.resource.data.page is string &&
        request.resource.data.page.size() <= 100 &&
        request.resource.data.message is string &&
        request.resource.data.message.size() <= 300 &&
        request.resource.data.stack is string &&
        request.resource.data.stack.size() <= 800 &&
        request.resource.data.appVersion is string &&
        request.resource.data.appVersion.size() <= 20 &&
        request.resource.data.userAgent is string &&
        request.resource.data.userAgent.size() <= 150 &&
        request.resource.data.resolved == false &&
        // Force the SERVER timestamp (serverTimestamp() resolves to request.time). Previously any
        // client value passed, so a flood of shape-valid junk could be BACKDATED to corrupt triage
        // in the admin Error Log. (Volume/abuse is the eventual App Check control — Track D — but
        // pinning the timestamp closes the forgery vector now.)
        request.resource.data.timestamp == request.time;
      allow read, update, delete: if
        request.auth != null && request.auth.token.admin == true;
    }

    match /circulars/{date} {
      // Open read: calendar-app.js reads Circular metadata without a Firebase Auth
      // session (the Calendar deliberately supports anonymous use, matching Huddles).
      // The stored URL is a BEARER download URL (a permanent tokenised Storage link):
      // anyone who obtains it can fetch the file while the token is valid — it is NOT
      // re-checked against Storage rules at click time. Don't store confidential documents
      // here unless the delivery model changes. (Metadata read being open is acceptable
      // because the bearer URL is the real access control, not the Firestore doc.)
      // Only admin may write; hasOnly (not hasAll) prevents extra fields.
      allow read;
      allow create, update: if
        request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.token.admin == true &&
        request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['date', 'storageUrl', 'storagePath', 'fileType', 'uploadedAt', 'uploadedBy']) &&
        request.resource.data.date is string &&
        request.resource.data.date.size() == 10 &&
        // Shape guard only: YYYY-MM-DD, month 01-12, day 01-31 (anchored). Does NOT reject a
        // well-formed but non-existent day (e.g. 2026-02-31); the client date paths prevent those.
        request.resource.data.date.matches('[0-9]{4}-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12][0-9]|3[01])') &&
        request.resource.data.storageUrl is string &&
        request.resource.data.fileType in ['pdf', 'docx'] &&
        request.resource.data.uploadedAt is timestamp &&
        request.resource.data.uploadedBy is string;
      allow delete: if request.auth != null && request.auth.token.admin == true;
    }

    match /newsletters/{date} {
      // Open read — same rationale as circulars above.
      allow read;
      allow create, update: if
        request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.token.admin == true &&
        request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['date', 'storageUrl', 'storagePath', 'fileType', 'uploadedAt', 'uploadedBy']) &&
        request.resource.data.date is string &&
        request.resource.data.date.size() == 10 &&
        // Shape guard only: YYYY-MM-DD, month 01-12, day 01-31 (anchored). Does NOT reject a
        // well-formed but non-existent day (e.g. 2026-02-31); the client date paths prevent those.
        request.resource.data.date.matches('[0-9]{4}-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12][0-9]|3[01])') &&
        request.resource.data.storageUrl is string &&
        request.resource.data.fileType in ['pdf', 'docx'] &&
        request.resource.data.uploadedAt is timestamp &&
        request.resource.data.uploadedBy is string;
      allow delete: if request.auth != null && request.auth.token.admin == true;
    }

    match /pushSubscriptions/{docId} {
      // No client read — Cloud Functions fan-out via Admin SDK and bypass these rules.
      // Removing client read prevents any authenticated/anonymous session from enumerating
      // push endpoints and encryption keys.
      allow read: if false;
      allow create, update: if
        request.auth != null &&
        // NOT the shared Calendar viewer (v20.12). Every office PC that unlocks with the staff PIN
        // signs in as the SAME uid, so a subscription written under it would be `owner`ed by an
        // identity fifty people share — any of them could then delete any other's, and the
        // subscription itself would sit on a shared machine pushing one person's Huddle to the mess
        // room with nobody able to turn it off. The client already declines to offer this in viewer
        // mode (calendar-app.js); this is the half that does not depend on the client being right.
        // Written as `!= true` rather than requiring a `name` claim so a stale cached client still
        // running the old anonymous bootstrap can keep renewing its subscription through the
        // mixed-version window.
        // The `in` guard is NOT optional. Reading an ABSENT custom claim is an evaluation ERROR in
        // Rules ("Property calendarViewer is undefined on object"), and inside an `&&` chain an
        // error denies the whole clause — so a bare `token.calendarViewer != true` broke every
        // ordinary subscription write, member and anonymous alike. It survives inside an `||` chain
        // (an error loses to a true branch), which is why the overrides READ rule above can use the
        // shorter form and this one cannot. Caught by the emulator suite, invisible to static checks.
        (!('calendarViewer' in request.auth.token) || request.auth.token.calendarViewer != true) &&
        // hasOnly prevents injecting extra fields (e.g. memberName, metadata). `owner` is
        // permitted (see below) but not required — older cached clients omit it.
        request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['endpoint', 'keys', 'subscribedAt', 'owner']) &&
        request.resource.data.endpoint is string &&
        request.resource.data.endpoint.size() > 0 &&
        request.resource.data.endpoint.size() < 2048 &&
        request.resource.data.keys is map &&
        // Restrict the nested keys map to exactly p256dh + auth — savePushSubscription
        // writes only these two (from PushSubscription.getKey).
        request.resource.data.keys.keys().hasOnly(['p256dh', 'auth']) &&
        request.resource.data.keys.p256dh is string &&
        request.resource.data.keys.p256dh.size() < 256 &&
        request.resource.data.keys.auth is string &&
        request.resource.data.keys.auth.size() < 64 &&
        request.resource.data.subscribedAt is timestamp &&
        // `owner` (Firebase Auth uid) is OPTIONAL for backward-compat, but WHEN PRESENT must be
        // the caller's own uid — so a session can only ever claim its own subscription. New
        // clients always write it; that's what lets the per-owner delete rule below protect them.
        (!('owner' in request.resource.data) ||
          (request.resource.data.owner is string && request.resource.data.owner == request.auth.uid)) &&
        // On UPDATE, also re-check the EXISTING doc's owner (mirrors the per-owner delete rule below).
        // Without this, a session that knew a doc id could OVERWRITE another user's subscription by
        // re-stamping its own uid as owner — the same "overwrite by re-labelling" gap that was closed
        // for overrides. `resource` is null on create (nothing to protect); a legacy owner-less doc
        // stays updatable so VAPID-rotation cleanup can't be locked out.
        (resource == null || !('owner' in resource.data) || resource.data.owner == request.auth.uid);
      // Delete YOUR OWN subscription (owner == your uid), OR a legacy doc that carries no owner
      // (older clients wrote none) — keeping those deletable so VAPID-rotation cleanup can't be
      // locked out. Orphans left by a uid change are swept server-side by fanOutPush's 410/404
      // cleanup, so nothing accumulates. (A5, F-SEC-5 — was `request.auth != null` for any id.)
      allow delete: if request.auth != null &&
        // Same `in` guard as create/update above, for the same reason.
        (!('calendarViewer' in request.auth.token) || request.auth.token.calendarViewer != true) &&
        (!('owner' in resource.data) || resource.data.owner == request.auth.uid);
    }

    // ── viewerAttempts — the staff-PIN throttle store (v20.12) ─────────────────────────────────
    // Written ONLY by the unlockCalendarViewer Cloud Function through the Admin SDK, which bypasses
    // these rules entirely. Closed to every client in both directions, and BOTH halves matter:
    // a client that could READ it would learn how close a source is to being blocked (which is how
    // an automated traversal would pace itself under the limit), and one that could WRITE it could
    // clear its own failure count between guesses, which would remove the limit altogether.
    match /viewerAttempts/{sourceKey} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // ── overtimeWindows — Overtime Availability (OVERTIME_AVAILABILITY.md) ─────────────────────
    //
    // DELIBERATELY THE SMALLEST RULESET IN THIS FILE, and the smallness is the design.
    //
    // Ordinary members never read Overtime data through Firestore at all — their one read path is
    // the `getMyOvertimeState` Cloud Function, which returns only the windows holding a frozen
    // participant record for the caller's canonical identity. Every mutation is likewise
    // server-owned (Admin SDK, bypassing these rules), because deadline, revision and identity
    // decisions cannot be expressed as a client write rule and must not be attempted here.
    //
    // So there are exactly two facts to encode: reviewers may read, and nobody may write.
    //
    // WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and why:
    //
    //  · A participant-based member-read rule. It was in an earlier draft "for defence in depth",
    //    and a rule no production client exercises is a rule that rots — written once, verified
    //    against a synthetic test, then quietly wrong. Add it only when a real client consumer
    //    needs it, and test it through that consumer.
    //
    //  · A `request.time < retentionUntil` retention filter. Two reasons. Rules are NOT filters:
    //    a condition on `resource.data` does not exclude a document from a list, it fails the whole
    //    query — so one unpurged expired window would blank a Manager's live view of a current one.
    //    And it could only ever constrain the reviewer, who is the one party entitled to the data;
    //    it protects it from nobody. Retention is enforced where it is visible and testable: both
    //    endpoints omit expired windows, so behaviour never depends on when the purge last ran.
    //
    // The recursive wildcard covers participants/, submissions/ and submissions/*/revisions/ — one
    // posture for the whole tree, which is correct here precisely because no sub-path has a
    // different reader. `calendarViewer` gets nothing: it holds no `name`, `admin` or `manager`
    // claim, so it fails this rule the same way an anonymous session does.
    match /overtimeWindows/{weekEnding}/{document=**} {
      allow read: if request.auth != null && (
        request.auth.token.admin == true ||
        request.auth.token.manager == true
      );
      allow write: if false;   // Admin SDK only — functions/overtime.js
    }

    match /analytics/{docId} {
      // Anonymous aggregate usage counters only — NO member identity is ever stored.
      // Page popularity lives in analytics/pv_<YYYY-MM> ({ month, counts{} }); active-
      // account counts live in analytics/activeAccounts ({ months{}, daily{} }). Uniqueness
      // is deduped client-side (usage-reporter.js) so the server only ever sees "+1".
      // Read is admin-only. Any authenticated session may increment — a member's named session,
      // or the shared staff-PIN viewer — which is why writes are not gated on a name/admin claim.
      // (This said "including the calendar's anonymous Firebase session" until v21.63; that
      // bootstrap was removed at v20.12 and now runs only under the CALENDAR_PIN_ACCESS=false
      // rollback path, so it must not be cited as the reason the rule is loose.) The SHAPE is validated:
      // pv_ docs are pinned to a pv_YYYY-MM id with month == the id suffix, counts keys are
      // limited to the six known page ids, and each count value must be an int; activeAccounts
      // is pinned to that id with months/daily maps. Firestore rules still can't enforce
      // increment-ONLY (a write could set an int to any value) or validate the date-keyed
      // sub-map keys — App Check is the eventual integrity control; the data is non-sensitive
      // aggregate counts. No client delete — daily-bucket pruning is a field-level update.
      allow read: if request.auth != null && request.auth.token.admin == true;
      // Anti-WIPE guard (review B4): the legit writers only INCREMENT (setDoc merge with FieldValue),
      // never remove keys. A malicious non-merge overwrite with empty maps passed the shape check and
      // ZEROED existing counters (worse than the "tamper one value" App-Check gap). So on UPDATE a
      // non-admin write may not remove existing map keys. Admin is excepted because the Operations Usage
      // card legitimately PRUNES stale activeAccounts.daily buckets (a key removal). Value tampering of a
      // still-present key remains App-Check territory; this closes the destructive wipe. (`resource` is
      // null on create — no prior keys to preserve.)
      allow create, update: if
        request.auth != null &&
        (
          // Page popularity: analytics/pv_YYYY-MM = { month: 'YYYY-MM', counts: { <pageId>: int } }
          (docId.matches('pv_[0-9]{4}-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])') &&
           request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['month', 'counts']) &&
           request.resource.data.month is string &&
           docId == 'pv_' + request.resource.data.month &&
           request.resource.data.counts is map &&
           // Six page-view ids + the document/guide OPEN ids (v18.20 — Huddle/Circular/Newsletter
           // opens and the guide opens share the same anonymous counter map; guide-staff and
           // guide-paycalc added v19.95, guide-rangers with the fifth guide at v20.05). The list
           // must hold one id per NAV_GUIDES entry — firestore-contract-parity.test.mjs checks it
           // both ways, so a guide added without an id here fails rather than counting nothing.
           request.resource.data.counts.keys().hasOnly(['calendar', 'admin', 'paycalc', 'operations', 'settings', 'links', 'overtime',
                                                        'huddle', 'circular', 'newsletter',
                                                        'guide-staff', 'guide-paycalc', 'guide-railcard', 'guide-fip', 'guide-rangers']) &&
           (!('calendar'   in request.resource.data.counts) || request.resource.data.counts.calendar   is int) &&
           (!('admin'      in request.resource.data.counts) || request.resource.data.counts.admin      is int) &&
           (!('paycalc'    in request.resource.data.counts) || request.resource.data.counts.paycalc    is int) &&
           (!('operations' in request.resource.data.counts) || request.resource.data.counts.operations is int) &&
           (!('settings'   in request.resource.data.counts) || request.resource.data.counts.settings   is int) &&
           (!('links'      in request.resource.data.counts) || request.resource.data.counts.links      is int) &&
           (!('overtime'   in request.resource.data.counts) || request.resource.data.counts.overtime   is int) &&
           (!('huddle'     in request.resource.data.counts) || request.resource.data.counts.huddle     is int) &&
           (!('circular'   in request.resource.data.counts) || request.resource.data.counts.circular   is int) &&
           (!('newsletter' in request.resource.data.counts) || request.resource.data.counts.newsletter is int) &&
           (!('guide-staff'    in request.resource.data.counts) || request.resource.data.counts['guide-staff']    is int) &&
           (!('guide-paycalc'  in request.resource.data.counts) || request.resource.data.counts['guide-paycalc']  is int) &&
           (!('guide-railcard' in request.resource.data.counts) || request.resource.data.counts['guide-railcard'] is int) &&
           (!('guide-fip'      in request.resource.data.counts) || request.resource.data.counts['guide-fip']      is int) &&
           (!('guide-rangers'  in request.resource.data.counts) || request.resource.data.counts['guide-rangers']  is int) &&
           (resource == null || request.auth.token.admin == true ||
            request.resource.data.counts.keys().hasAll(resource.data.counts.keys()))) ||
          // Active-account counters: analytics/activeAccounts = { months?: {…}, daily?: {…} }
          (docId == 'activeAccounts' &&
           request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['months', 'daily']) &&
           (!('months' in request.resource.data) || request.resource.data.months is map) &&
           (!('daily'  in request.resource.data) || request.resource.data.daily  is map) &&
           (resource == null || request.auth.token.admin == true ||
            ((!('months' in resource.data) || ('months' in request.resource.data && request.resource.data.months.keys().hasAll(resource.data.months.keys()))) &&
             (!('daily'  in resource.data) || ('daily'  in request.resource.data && request.resource.data.daily.keys().hasAll(resource.data.daily.keys())))))) ||
          // Per-ADDRESS counters (v19.23): analytics/origins = { daily: { 'YYYY-MM-DD|<origin>[|pwa]': int } }.
          // Which of the two served addresses each account is actually using, and whether from the
          // installed app — the migration metric. Anonymous: uniqueness is deduped client-side, so the
          // server only ever sees "+1". A SEPARATE doc from activeAccounts on purpose: that one is
          // pinned to hasOnly(['months','daily']) and rules evaluate the RESULTING document, so adding
          // a key there would have denied every later write — including the counters that already work
          // — for as long as the rules deploy lagged the hosting deploy. Same anti-wipe guard as the
          // others; the key set is unbounded here (dates × origins) so it cannot be enumerated, which
          // is why the client keeps origins to a closed label set (usage-stats.originLabel).
          (docId == 'origins' &&
           request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['daily']) &&
           request.resource.data.daily is map &&
           (resource == null || request.auth.token.admin == true ||
            request.resource.data.daily.keys().hasAll(resource.data.daily.keys()))) ||
          // Page-load latency (Project 0): analytics/perf_YYYY-MM = { month: 'YYYY-MM', samples: {…} }.
          // Anonymous bucketed counters keyed by non-identifying dimensions (perf-stats.perfSampleKey);
          // the dynamic sample values can't be validated here (App Check is the eventual integrity
          // control — non-sensitive aggregate counts), but key REMOVAL (a wipe) is blocked below.
          (docId.matches('perf_[0-9]{4}-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])') &&
           request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['month', 'samples']) &&
           request.resource.data.month is string &&
           docId == 'perf_' + request.resource.data.month &&
           request.resource.data.samples is map &&
           (resource == null || request.auth.token.admin == true ||
            request.resource.data.samples.keys().hasAll(resource.data.samples.keys())))
        );
      allow delete: if false;
    }

  }
}
